Z||000000||jasonl||01-26-2001||01:58 PM||||I have been desperatly trying to get rid of this code 32.  I have changed the egr valve, solenoid and working on the tubing.  I found some 5/16" steel brake line at Advance Auto and was wondering if I could use that in place of the original 3/16" plastic tubing?  Also I'm not quite sure where this egr tube is everyone is talking about. Can someone help? (Please!) ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||01-26-2001||03:40 PM||||EGR tube goes from the bottom of the intake manifold over to the side of the egr valve housing (I think).  It is usually wrapped in some sort of silvery fabric like material.  New ones cost ~$80 or so.||||reg||3
Z||000002||My7Fieros||01-26-2001||03:42 PM||||Im assuming you have a V6. Then chances are more than likely its your EGR tube..thats usually what it is.<BR>Look on the engine at the large red intake on the top that has Fiero written on it. Look to the left part (where it narrows to go into the throttle body). Underneath youll see a silver tube that may or may not have a cloth type wrapping around it. Follow it to the EGR sylenoid)...(or just look to the 2:00 position kinda under the distributor). Wiggle the tube, Id betcha its broken.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierosound||01-26-2001||05:36 PM||||Take the EGR tube of and inspect it.  Even if it's not broken, it may be gunked up.  I found this to be the case when I removed mine.||||reg||10
Z||000004||fierosound||01-26-2001||05:37 PM||||...<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000005||Tom88gt||01-26-2001||08:14 PM||||STOP!!!  Don't waste the time I did...<P>Check the basics first.  Check that you have a good vacuum source from the throttle body-EGR valve line.  Use a vacuum guage.  There will be little or no vacuum at idle.  Move the throttle by hand and you should get vacuum.  <P>Check for 12v power at the wiring harness with ignition on.  Check that the ground wires have continuity when you ground the ECM.  <P>Check the solenoid for continuity (about 90 ohms)<P>Make sure the EGR valve diaphram holds vacuum.  The valve side and EGR tube are not linked to the EGR solenoid and the ECM doesn't care as long as the valve holds vacuum.<P>Check the items that are directly conected to the EGR solenoid.<P>I checked all the other stuff and bought an expensive valve, only to still have the code.  the problem was nothing more than a blocked vacuum line.<P>Vacuum leaks and electrical shorts are the number 1 cause of problems on modern EPA engines.<P>||||reg||4
Z||000006||mrfiero||01-26-2001||10:00 PM||||A broken EGR tube won't set a code 32.......it will set a code 35 (IAC).  Look at the EGR vacuum solenoid......it's on the passenger side of the engine compartment and has a 4-pin plug and vacuum lines on both ends.  Look closely at the rubber vacuum line that runs from the solenoid to the firewall......more times than not this line is either missing or cracked.  Replace it and you should be good to go.<P>If that doesn't help then inspect/replace the plastic vacuum lines under the plenum and/or replace the vacuum solenoid.||||reg||1
